===========================================================================
VERDICT: PASS on 3 of the 4 assertions; the alternate-path step is UNMEASURED.
===========================================================================

PASS -- "Configure the DUT to be the root / DUT should become the root bridge"
    spanning-tree priority 0 on the DUT, partners left at default 32768.
    DUT:  Root Id 0000:0000cd370d6f == Bridge Id 0000:0000cd370d6f
    The Root Id is the stack's VIRTUAL MAC, so the whole VCStack is the root
    bridge, not one member.

PASS -- "Check other switches have detected the DUT as the Root"
    AR4050S : Root Id 0000:0000cd370d6f, Bridge Id 8000:0000cd400394,
              po1        = Rootport / Forwarding
    x230    : Root Id 0000:0000cd370d6f, Bridge Id 8000:001aeb91cca1,
              port1.0.3  = Rootport / Forwarding
    Both partners independently elected a root port pointing at the DUT and
    agree on its bridge ID. All three agree.

PASS -- "Traffic should be received on the DUT"
    tb470 eth2 -> DUT -> x230 -> eth1 : 10/10 with the ring closed.

UNMEASURED -- "Shut down the link between sw1 and the DUT / traffic should take
the alternate path"
    There is no single-broadcast-domain alternate path on this bench. The
    physical ring spans three different VLANs:
        DUT port1.0.2  -- x230 port1.0.3    vlan 1 (DUT) <-> vlan 100 (x230)
        DUT po1        -- 4050 port1.0.4    vlan 10, the routed transit
        x230 port1.0.2 -- 4050 port1.0.2    vlan 100 <-> native vlan 10
    The two legs meet the DUT in DIFFERENT VLANs (1 and 10), which do not
    bridge on the DUT, so cutting the x230 leg leaves no layer-2 detour. Making
    it one domain would merge the routed vlan10 transit into vlan1 and change
    the bench's addressing -- beyond this case, and it would invalidate the
    L3 topology the other groups rely on.

=========================== SAFETY FINDING =================================
**A physical link can forward on a path spanning tree does not know about,
because LACP global-passive-mode silently puts it in an aggregation.**

When the ring was closed, NO port anywhere reported Discarding -- a loop with
no blocking port. Cause:
    4050# show running-config interface port1.0.2
     channel-group 2 mode passive        <-- auto-added, never configured by hand
The 4050 carried `lacp global-passive-mode enable`, so bringing the port up
auto-enrolled it into po2. Spanning tree runs on the AGGREGATOR, and po2 was
not up, so STP had no port it could block while the physical link forwarded.

The identical thing happened on the STACK on 2026-09-21 (a freed port
auto-rejoined as `channel-group 2 mode passive`, and `switchport atmf-link` was
then refused on an "aggregator member"). It has now bitten on two different
devices.

ACTIONS TAKEN: ring re-opened immediately on detection, before any traffic test
was trusted; `no lacp global-passive-mode enable` on the AR4050S; the port
taken out of the channel-group. The stack already had passive mode disabled.

RULE FOR ANY RING WORK ON THIS BENCH: before closing a redundant leg, confirm
the port appears in `show spanning-tree brief` AS A PORT with a real state --
not merely that "Spanning Tree Enabled" is reported. If the leg is inside an
aggregator that is down, STP cannot block it.
